      { label: "The diminutive is politeness, not size", body: "“Suavecito”, “momentico”, “ahorita”, “tintico” — Colombian Spanish adds -ito far more than most dialects, and it usually softens the sentence rather than shrinking the thing. A “tintico” is not a smaller coffee; it is a friendlier way to say coffee. Refusing to use diminutives makes correct Spanish sound colder than intended." },
      { label: "Greeting comes before business", body: "Walking in and naming what you want, with no greeting first, reads as rude in a way that is easy to underestimate. The greeting is doing social work: it acknowledges the person before the transaction. This holds in shops, taxis, clinics and phone calls." },
      { label: "“Regalar” is a softener, not a claim on free things", body: "The literal meaning worries learners, and it worries them in the wrong direction. No one thinks you are asking for a gift. It functions the way English “could I get” does — a request dressed as a smaller imposition than it is." },
      { label: "The tienda is an institution", body: "A tienda de barrio sells coffee, eggs, single cigarettes, phone airtime and beer, often on informal credit for regulars. Being known at your tienda is a real social position. The language is warm because the relationship is expected to last, not because Colombians are being nice to a stranger." }
